# Sparkline

The [[chart]] island is a Svelte bar chart of lead scores. This file is its
companion: `:sparkline`, a **SolidJS** client island that draws a tiny trend
line — the rolling movement of the pipeline's average score over recent weaves.
Two different frameworks, one workbook, no conflict. That is the point of this
file. A client island is **placement = client**: it runs in the browser, hydrated
from the static bundle or shipped as an island under nexus SSR. The framework it
happens to be written in is an implementation detail the weave erases.

When you author a client unit you put the language word in front of the block.
Svelte units say `client svelte`; this one says `client solid`. The weave reads
that word, compiles the unit with the right toolchain, and emits a hydrating
island. Both islands land in the same `salesapp.html`; both mount into their own
shadow boundary; neither knows the other exists. Solid's fine-grained reactivity
and Svelte's compiled reactivity coexist because the workbook never asks them to
share a runtime — they share a *page*, the oldest contract on the web.

## What it renders

`:sparkline` takes a list of recent average scores — one number per weave run —
and draws a single SVG `polyline`, plus a dot on the most recent point and a
caption with the latest value and its delta. No axes, no legend; a sparkline is a
word-sized graphic that lives inline with prose, the way this one lives inline
with the dashboard. The values arrive from the server as a plain list of
integers; for a static bundle they are baked in at weave time, and under live SSR
they stream in over RCP and the island re-renders.

The data shape is deliberately small: a list of `integer`, oldest first. The
caller passes the trail of pipeline averages computed by [[pipeline]] after each
run, so a reader watching the dashboard sees the pipeline's health move.

client solid :sparkline do
  import { createSignal, createMemo, onMount, For } from "solid-js"
  import { render } from "solid-js/web"

  // A Solid component. Props arrive from the host as a plain object; `scores`
  // is the trail of average lead scores, oldest first, e.g. [58, 61, 60, 67, 70].
  function Sparkline(props) {
    const [scores, setScores] = createSignal(props.scores ?? [])

    // The host re-pushes data under live SSR; expose a setter so the island
    // mount point can feed new trails without a remount.
    onMount(() => {
      if (props.subscribe) props.subscribe(setScores)
    })

    const width = 120
    const height = 32
    const pad = 3

    // Fine-grained derived state: each memo recomputes only when scores() moves.
    const bounds = createMemo(() => {
      const s = scores()
      if (s.length === 0) return { lo: 0, hi: 1 }
      const lo = Math.min(...s)
      const hi = Math.max(...s)
      return { lo, hi: hi === lo ? lo + 1 : hi }
    })

    // Map each score to an (x, y) point inside the box. y is flipped because
    // SVG's origin is top-left and a higher score should sit higher up.
    const points = createMemo(() => {
      const s = scores()
      const { lo, hi } = bounds()
      const span = hi - lo
      const stepX = s.length > 1 ? (width - pad * 2) / (s.length - 1) : 0
      return s.map((v, i) => {
        const x = pad + i * stepX
        const y = height - pad - ((v - lo) / span) * (height - pad * 2)
        return { x, y, v }
      })
    })

    const path = createMemo(() =>
      points().map((p) => `${p.x.toFixed(1)},${p.y.toFixed(1)}`).join(" ")
    )

    const latest = createMemo(() => {
      const s = scores()
      return s.length ? s[s.length - 1] : 0
    })

    // Delta vs the previous run drives the caption's sign and colour.
    const delta = createMemo(() => {
      const s = scores()
      return s.length > 1 ? s[s.length - 1] - s[s.length - 2] : 0
    })

    const trend = createMemo(() => (delta() >= 0 ? "up" : "down"))

    return (
      <figure class="sparkline" data-trend={trend()}>
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${width} ${height}`} role="img"
             aria-label={`Pipeline score trend, latest ${latest()}`}>
          <polyline points={path()} fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                    stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
          <For each={points().slice(-1)}>
            {(p) => <circle cx={p.x} cy={p.y} r="2.4" fill="currentColor" />}
          </For>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          {latest()} <span class="delta">{delta() >= 0 ? "+" : ""}{delta()}</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    )
  }

  // Island entry point: the weave calls this with the mount node and props.
  export function mount(el, props) {
    return render(() => Sparkline(props), el)
  }
end

## How the trail gets here

The numbers are not invented in the browser. After each run, [[pipeline]] folds
its ranked leads into an average score and appends it to a typed trail held on
the server. The trail is just `data :score_trail, of: integer` — values of a
structure, queryable, rendered by default. The island receives a snapshot of it.

server :score_trail_feed do
  # Average score of the current ranked board, appended after a pipeline run.
  # Pulls the same ranked leads :chart bars, so both islands tell one story.
  def average(leads) do
    case leads do
      [] -> 0
      _ ->
        total = Enum.reduce(leads, 0, fn %Lead{score: s}, acc -> acc + s end)
        round(total / length(leads))
    end
  end

  # The trail is the document's store: read the last N, push the newest.
  def trail(history, leads, keep \\ 12) do
    (history ++ [average(leads)])
    |> Enum.take(-keep)
  end
end

Under a **static bundle** the weave evaluates `score_trail_feed.trail/3` at build
time over the seed [[leads]] and bakes the resulting list into the island's
props — the sparkline is correct the moment the file opens, with zero server.
Under **nexus SSR** the same function runs on the BEAM per request. Under **live
SSR** the server pushes a fresh trail over the RCP websocket each time
[[pipeline]] completes, the island's `subscribe` callback fires `setScores`, and
Solid's memos recompute only the points that moved. One authoring model, three
delivery modes, no rewrite — see [[weave]] for how the gate wires it.

## Why two frameworks is fine, not a smell

A common objection: shipping Svelte *and* SolidJS bloats the bundle. In practice
the weave tree-shakes each island against its own framework and dedupes shared
chunks, so you pay for what an island uses, not for the union of every framework
present. More importantly, the choice is **local**: the author of `:chart` picked
Svelte, the author of this file picked Solid, and neither decision leaked. That is
the open-composition stance — the workbook adds reachable compositions rather than
forcing one runtime on every island. The `work check` graph (see [[code-graph]])
records each island's framework as a node attribute, so `work near :sparkline`
shows it sitting beside `:chart` with a shared dataflow edge from [[pipeline]],
not a dependency on it.

Read this island next to [[chart]]: same input lineage, different visual idiom,
different framework, one page. The dashboard composes both without either knowing
the other is there.
